@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

@cindex what Harmless is
Harmless is a coding harness written in Emacs Lisp.  It is not gptel.
It does not wrap Grok Build, Claude Code, or the Codex CLI.  It talks
to a model provider itself, keeps the transcript in Emacs, and runs
tools in the project directory.

A session is one conversation.  It is pinned to one connection and one
model.  Several sessions can run in one Emacs.  One can use Grok,
another a local model, and a third Claude, each in its own project.

The transcript buffer is a view.  The saved session on disk is the
record.  Closing the buffer does not delete the session.

Harmless requires GNU Emacs 31.

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

@findex harmless-setup
Put the @file{lisp} directory on @code{load-path} and load the package.

@example
(add-to-list 'load-path "~/programming/emacs/harmless/lisp")
(require 'harmless)
@end example

@vindex harmless-providers
@vindex harmless-default-model
@vindex harmless-default-reasoning-effort
@vindex harmless-default-provider-name
Choose the connections Harmless may use.  Most people need one.

@example
(setq harmless-providers (list (harmless-make-xai))
      harmless-default-model "grok-4.6"
      harmless-default-reasoning-effort "xhigh")
@end example

@findex harmless-make-xai
@findex harmless-make-openai
@findex harmless-make-anthropic
@findex harmless-make-openai-compat
The constructors are @code{harmless-make-xai},
@code{harmless-make-openai}, @code{harmless-make-anthropic}, and
@code{harmless-make-openai-compat}.  Each takes an optional connection
name.  The default names are @samp{xAI}, @samp{OpenAI}, and
@samp{Anthropic}.  A second account needs its own name, such as
@samp{xAI work}.

@vindex harmless-directory
Settings can live in your init file or in @file{config.el} under
@code{harmless-directory}.  That directory defaults to @file{harmless}
inside @code{user-emacs-directory}, so a typical path is
@file{~/.emacs.d/harmless/config.el}.  Harmless loads @file{config.el}
once, the first time a command needs it.

If @code{harmless-providers} is empty, @kbd{M-x harmless} asks you to
pick a provider and a connection name.

A local OpenAI-compatible server looks like this:

@example
(setq harmless-providers
      (list (harmless-make-openai-compat
             "local"
             :host "127.0.0.1:11434"
             :protocol "http"
             :endpoint "/v1/chat/completions"
             :key "none"
             :models '("llama3.2"))))
@end example

@node Starting
@chapter Starting

@findex harmless
@kindex M-x harmless
@kbd{M-x harmless} opens the session for the current project.  A
prefix argument opens the dashboard instead.

The current project is the project root Emacs would use for the
buffer, or @code{default-directory} when the buffer is not in a
project.  The directory must be absolute.  Harmless refuses to treat a
missing directory as the filesystem root.

On the first run, sign in when the provider asks.  @xref{Accounts}.

@node Sessions
@chapter Sessions

@findex harmless-current
@findex harmless-new
@findex harmless-chat
@findex harmless-switch
@findex harmless-dashboard
@cindex session
@cindex detached chat

@table @kbd
@item M-x harmless-current
Open or resume the newest session for this project.  If none exists,
start one with the default connection and model.

@item M-x harmless-new
Start a new session in the current project.  Harmless asks for the
connection, the model, and the reasoning effort when more than one
choice exists.

@item M-x harmless-chat
Start a chat with no project and no working directory.  This is a
detached chat.  It can use global memory and ask you a question.  It
cannot read project files, run a shell, or enter plan mode.

@item M-x harmless-switch
Jump to a live or saved session.  Completion shows the project, the
title, and the session id.

@item M-x harmless-dashboard
List every session, grouped by project.  @xref{Keys}, for the
dashboard keys.
@end table

The first user message becomes the title, cut at 60 characters, until
you set a title when you create the session.  A set title stays.

The header line of the transcript shows the title (or the project
name), the connection, the model, the status, and the word
@samp{plan} while plan mode is on.  @xref{Plan mode}.

Two sessions in one project get separate transcript buffers.  The
buffer name is @code{*harmless: @var{project}*} when the project has
one session, and @code{*harmless: @var{project} @var{id}*} when it has
more.  A detached chat always includes the id.

@node Transcript
@chapter Transcript

@findex harmless-session-mode
@kindex TAB @r{(transcript)}
@kindex S-TAB @r{(transcript)}
@kindex LEFT @r{(transcript)}
@kindex RIGHT @r{(transcript)}
The transcript is read-only.  Replies are Markdown.  A tool call is a
folded line.  A run of reads and searches folds into one
@samp{explored} line.

@table @kbd
@item TAB
Toggle the fold at point.

@item S-TAB
Toggle every fold.

@item LEFT
Close the fold at point.

@item RIGHT
Open the fold at point.

@item RET
Move to the prompt.

@item i
Move to the prompt.

@item q
Quit the window.

@item k
Compact the transcript.
@end table

@findex harmless-compact
@vindex harmless-compact-keep-turns
@kindex k @r{(transcript)}
@kbd{k}, or @kbd{M-x harmless-compact}, summarizes the older turns and
replaces them with one summary.  The recent turns stay in the
transcript.  A turn starts at a user message and includes the
assistant reply and tool results that follow it.  The default keeps
the last two turns (@code{harmless-compact-keep-turns}).  A numeric
prefix argument sets how many turns to keep, and the command keeps at
least one.  An empty summary leaves the transcript as it was.  A turn
that is streaming or waiting on permission is left alone.  The same
command is @kbd{k} in the Harmless menu.

Click the model name in the header line to change the connection, the
model, and the effort.  @xref{Models}.

@node Prompt
@chapter Prompt

@findex harmless-prompt-send
@findex harmless-prompt-mode
@kindex C-c C-c
@kindex C-c RET
The prompt is a small window under the transcript.  Type there and
send the turn.

@table @kbd
@item C-c C-c
Send the prompt.  @kbd{C-c @key{RET}} does the same.

@item C-c C-a
Abort the in-flight turn or shell.  @xref{Trouble}.

@item C-c C-k
Open the Harmless menu.  @xref{Keys}.
@end table

An empty prompt is refused.  While a permission question is showing,
send is refused until you answer it.  @xref{Permissions}.

@node Permissions
@chapter Permissions

@findex harmless-set-permission-mode
@vindex harmless-permission-mode
@cindex permission
@kindex y @r{(permission)}
@kindex n @r{(permission)}
@kindex ! @r{(permission)}
Each tool belongs to a class: @code{read}, @code{edit}, or
@code{shell}.  A read never asks.  An edit asks.  A shell asks.

The permission mode of a session changes that.

@table @code
@item ask
The default.  Edits and shell commands ask.

@item accept-edits
Edits proceed.  Shell commands still ask.

@item always-approve
Edits and shell commands proceed.
@end table

@kbd{M-x harmless-set-permission-mode} sets the mode for the current
session.  The menu key is @kbd{p}.  New sessions take the value of
@code{harmless-permission-mode}, which defaults to @code{ask}.

When a tool needs a decision, the prompt shows the class and a short
description of the call.  For an edit, that description is the path.
For a shell command, it is the command.

@table @kbd
@item y
Allow this call.

@item n
Deny this call.  The model receives an error and can try something else.

@item !
Allow this class for the rest of the session.
@end table

Those three keys insert themselves when no question is pending, so you
can type them in an ordinary prompt.

A denied call does not change the project.  An allowed edit or shell
command runs at once.

@node Accounts
@chapter Accounts

@findex harmless-login
@findex harmless-logout
@kindex M-x harmless-login
@kindex M-x harmless-logout
@kbd{M-x harmless-login} signs in.  If several connections exist, it
asks which one.  @kbd{M-x harmless-logout} forgets the Harmless token
for that connection.  It does not delete a token that belongs to Grok
Build, Claude Code, or Codex.

@heading xAI

@vindex harmless-xai-use-grok-auth
Browser login opens @code{auth.x.ai}.  Harmless listens on port 56121.
Tokens are stored under @code{harmless-directory}, mode 0600, and
refresh on their own.  A prefix argument uses the device-code flow.
Harmless also uses that flow when port 56121 is already taken, for
example by Grok Build.

If you have already run @code{grok login} and
@code{harmless-xai-use-grok-auth} is non-nil, Harmless reuses
@file{~/.grok/auth.json} until you sign in or out from Emacs.  It
never writes that file.  A refresh is saved under
@code{harmless-directory} instead.

An API key still works when no OAuth session is active.  Put it in
@file{~/.authinfo} as @code{machine api.x.ai login apikey password
xai-...}, or export @code{XAI_API_KEY}.

@heading OpenAI

@vindex harmless-openai-use-codex-auth
Login opens ChatGPT OAuth and listens on @code{localhost:1455}.  A
prefix argument asks you to paste the redirect URL.  Harmless also
does that when port 1455 is busy.  Tokens go in
@file{auth-openai.json}.  A still-valid @file{~/.codex/auth.json} is
reused and never written.

ChatGPT subscription tokens are sent to the Codex Responses API, not
to @code{api.openai.com}.  @code{OPENAI_API_KEY} remains a usage-based
fallback on Chat Completions.

@heading Anthropic

@vindex harmless-anthropic-use-claude-auth
Login uses Claude Code's OAuth client.  The browser shows a code.
Paste it at the minibuffer prompt.  Tokens go in
@file{auth-anthropic.json}.  A still-valid
@file{~/.claude/.credentials.json} access token is reused.  Harmless
does not write or refresh that file.

@heading Several accounts

Give each account its own connection name.  The default names keep the
original files: @file{auth.json}, @file{auth-openai.json}, and
@file{auth-anthropic.json}.  Any other name gets
@file{auth-@var{slug}.json}.  A session stays on the connection it was
given.  One buffer can use a personal Grok account while another uses
a work Claude account.

A local server has no browser login.

@node Models
@chapter Models

@findex harmless-pick-model
@findex harmless-set-model
@findex harmless-set-reasoning-effort
@vindex harmless-default-model
@vindex harmless-default-reasoning-effort
@cindex reasoning effort
Click the model in the header line, or run @kbd{M-x harmless-pick-model}.
Harmless asks for a logged-in connection, then a model, then a
reasoning effort.  A provider with one model skips the model prompt.
A model that does not take an effort skips the effort prompt.

You can also set the pieces separately.  @kbd{M-x harmless-set-model}
changes the model.  @kbd{M-x harmless-set-reasoning-effort} changes
the effort.  An empty effort clears the session's own value and falls
back to @code{harmless-default-reasoning-effort}.

Models whose names start with @code{grok-4}, @code{gpt-5}, or
@code{gpt-6}, and Claude models, accept an effort.  The usual values
are @code{low}, @code{medium}, @code{high}, and @code{xhigh}.  Claude
also accepts @code{max}.  A model id such as @code{grok-4.6-xhigh}
sets both the model and the effort.

Nil for the default effort omits the parameter.  For grok-4.6, the
provider then uses @code{high}.

The choice is saved with the session.  The next turn uses it.

@node Tools
@chapter Tools

@cindex tools
The model does not change your files by printing a diff.  It calls
tools.  You see each call in the transcript.  Permission rules are in
@ref{Permissions}.

Paths are relative to the project root unless you pass an absolute
path that is still inside that root.  A path that escapes the project
is refused.  An empty path is refused.  The filesystem root is not a
project: a search there is refused, and Harmless will not store its
own state there.

A detached chat is offered @code{memory_read},
@code{memory_remember}, @code{memory_write_topic}, and
@code{ask_user}.  @xref{Memory}.  @xref{Questions}.

@menu
* File tools::  Read, list, write, replace, and patch.
* Search::      Find files and lines.
* Shell::       Run a command.
* Questions::   Ask one question with choices.
@end menu

@node File tools
@section File tools

@table @code
@item read_file
Read a project file.  @code{offset} is the first line, counting from
1.  @code{limit} is how many lines to return.  The result is cut at
@code{harmless-read-file-max-bytes} (100000).  A cut result ends with
@samp{[truncated]}.

@item list_dir
List the names in a directory.  A directory name ends with a slash.
The default path is the project root.

@item write_file
Create or replace a file with the given contents.  This is an edit.

@item replace
Replace one unique string in a file.  If the string occurs more than
once, the call fails unless @code{replace_all} is true.  This is an
edit.

@item apply_patch
Apply several hunks in one call.  Each hunk names a @code{path}, an
@code{old_string}, and a @code{new_string}.  The old string must occur
once in that file.  Later hunks on the same file see earlier
replacements.  If any hunk fails, no file is changed.  This is an edit.

In plan mode, @code{write_file}, @code{replace}, and @code{apply_patch}
fail.  The model must write @file{plan.md} with @code{write_plan}.
@xref{Plan mode}.
@end table

@node Search
@section Search

@table @code
@item glob
Find files whose names match a glob.  The pattern may be a basename
or a relative path.

@item grep
Search file contents for a regular expression.  The optional
arguments are @code{path}, @code{glob}, @code{engine}, @code{context},
@code{type}, and @code{multiline}.
@end table

@cindex ripgrep
@cindex ag
@cindex grep
@code{path} is a directory inside the project.  A trailing slash is
the same directory.  @code{glob} limits basenames, for example
@code{*.el}.

@code{engine} is @code{rg}, @code{ag}, or @code{elisp}.  With no
engine, Harmless uses ripgrep if @command{rg} is on @code{exec-path},
then ag if @command{ag} is on @code{exec-path}, then an Emacs Lisp
scan.

Ripgrep and ag skip ignored files and the @file{.git} directory.
They do search hidden files, so a file such as @file{.harmless} is
visible.  The Emacs Lisp scan reads every regular file under the
directory, including ignored files and @file{.git}.

@code{context} is the number of lines to show around each hit.  A hit
is printed as @code{path:line:text}.  A context line is printed as
@code{path-line-text}.

@code{type} is a file type that the chosen engine knows.  Ripgrep
types come from @code{rg --type-list}.  Ag types come from
@code{ag --list-file-types}.  The Emacs Lisp scan has no file types.

@code{multiline} lets the pattern cross lines.  Only ripgrep can do
that.  Ag refuses it.  The Emacs Lisp scan refuses it.

The pattern is required, and it is case-sensitive.  An ag pattern
cannot start with a hyphen.  A long result is cut at the same byte
cap as @code{read_file}.

@node Shell
@section Shell

@vindex harmless-shell-timeout-seconds
@vindex harmless-shell-output-max-bytes
@code{run_shell} runs one command in the project root through your
shell.  Each command starts there.  A @code{cd} in one command does
not change the next command.

The command is killed after @code{harmless-shell-timeout-seconds}
(120).  The text returned to the model, and shown in the transcript,
is cut at @code{harmless-shell-output-max-bytes} (20000).  A timeout
is an error, followed by whatever output was captured.

Plan mode does not inspect the command.  A shell command can still
write files while a plan is being written.  @xref{Plan mode}.

@node Questions
@section Questions

@cindex question
@code{ask_user} asks one question.  @code{question} is the prompt.
@code{choices} are the answers.  Emacs asks with completion, and the
answer must be one of those choices.  The tool returns
@samp{The user chose: } followed by that answer.

A missing or blank question is refused.  A missing choice list, an
empty list, a blank choice, a choice that spans lines, and a repeated
choice are refused.  A choice is text.  A value such as @code{/} or
@code{lisp/} is returned as that text.  Cancelling the prompt returns
@samp{The user cancelled the question.}

The tool does not ask for permission first.  A detached chat can use
it.  Plan mode can use it.

@node Instructions
@chapter Instructions

@cindex project instructions
@cindex AGENTS.md
@cindex HARMLESS.md
@cindex CLAUDE.md
@vindex harmless-claude-md
Harmless reads these names in each directory, from your home directory
down to the session directory:

@enumerate
@item
@file{CLAUDE.md}, when @code{harmless-claude-md} says to read it

@item
@file{AGENTS.md}

@item
@file{HARMLESS.md}

@item
@file{.harmless/HARMLESS.md}
@end enumerate

@code{harmless-claude-md} has three values.  @code{nil} never reads
@file{CLAUDE.md}.  @code{t} always reads it.  @code{anthropic}, the
default, reads it only when the session model id starts with
@code{claude-}.  A model such as @code{grok-4.6} does not match.
@file{CLAUDE.md} is read before the other three names, so those later
files win when they disagree.

A file applies to its directory and everything under it.  Outer
directories come first.  At one directory, the files are read in the
order above.  When two files disagree, the later one wins.  Empty
files are skipped.  The walk stops at your home directory and at the
filesystem root.

An instruction file can include another file by writing
@code{@@path}.  The path is relative to the file that contains the
import, not the session directory.  A backslash before a space is part
of the path.  The file's text replaces the import.  An import inside
inline code or a fenced code block is left as written.  A quoted path
is left as written.  The file must stay inside the session directory.
Harmless refuses an empty path, a missing file, a directory, a cycle,
and a path that leaves the session.  The filesystem root is not a
directory an import may enter.

The text is sent with every turn.  It is not copied into the
transcript.  A detached chat does not walk @code{default-directory}
looking for these files.

@node Skills
@chapter Skills

@cindex skills
@cindex SKILL.md
A skill is a directory that contains @file{SKILL.md}.  The file starts
with @code{name} and @code{description} in YAML frontmatter.  If the
name is missing, Harmless uses the directory name.  If the description
is missing, it uses the first paragraph of the body.

Each turn receives a catalog: the name, the description, and the path.
The body is not sent until the model reads that file with
@code{read_file}.

Harmless looks for a @file{skills} directory inside these vendor
directories, from your home directory down to the session:

@table @file
@item .agents/skills
@itemx .codex/skills
Codex.

@item .claude/skills
Claude Code.

@item .grok/skills
Grok Build.

@item .harmless/skills
Harmless.
@end table

In one directory, a later entry in that list replaces an earlier skill
of the same name.  A skill closer to the session replaces one of the
same name farther away.  @file{/etc/codex/skills} is included when it
exists, at the lowest priority.  Skills that ship inside the Codex
application, and are not files on disk, are not read.

@node Memory
@chapter Memory

@findex harmless-remember
@findex harmless-dream
@cindex memory
Memory is Markdown under @code{harmless-directory}, in @file{memory}.
Global notes apply to every project.  Workspace notes belong to one
project.  A detached chat has workspace memory unavailable and global
memory available.

Each scope has three directories:

@table @file
@item topics
Curated notes, one file per topic.

@item observations/_inbox
New facts, not yet folded into a topic.

@item archive
Inbox files that @code{harmless-dream} has folded.
@end table

@file{MEMORY.md} in each scope is a generated index.  A turn receives
the index, not the notes.

@table @kbd
@item M-x harmless-remember
Save a title and a note in workspace memory.  A prefix argument saves
it in global memory.

@item M-x harmless-dream
Fold the inbox into topics for this project and for global memory.
The inbox files move to @file{archive}.
@end table

The model uses three tools.

@table @code
@item memory_read
Read one memory file.  @code{scope} is @code{workspace} or
@code{global}.

@item memory_remember
Append a fact to the inbox.  @code{topic} is an optional slug, such as
@code{emacs}.

@item memory_write_topic
Replace one topic file with the full Markdown you supply.
@end table

@node Plan mode
@chapter Plan mode

@findex harmless-plan
@findex enter_plan_mode
@findex write_plan
@findex exit_plan_mode
@cindex plan mode
@kbd{M-x harmless-plan} turns plan mode on for the current session.  A
prefix argument turns it off.  The menu key is @kbd{P}.  The model may
also call @code{enter_plan_mode}, which asks you before it switches.

While plan mode is on, the model is told to explore with read-only
tools, to write the plan only with @code{write_plan}, and to call
@code{exit_plan_mode} when the plan is ready.  @code{write_file},
@code{replace}, and @code{apply_patch} fail.  The header line shows
@samp{plan}.
@code{ask_user} can still ask a question.  @xref{Questions}.

@code{write_plan} writes @file{plan.md} in the session directory.  The
contents replace the whole file.  An empty plan is refused.

@code{exit_plan_mode} shows the plan and asks:

@example
Plan: a approve and build, s request changes, q quit
@end example

@table @kbd
@item a
Turn plan mode off and tell the model to implement the plan.

@item s
Ask for revision notes, leave plan mode on, and send those notes back.

@item q
Turn plan mode off and tell the model to stop without changing project
files.
@end table

Approving does not apply a patch by itself.  The same turn continues,
and later tool calls do the edits.

Shell commands are not classified.  In plan mode a shell command can
still write.  @xref{Shell}.

The flag is saved with the session.

@node Usage
@chapter Usage

@findex harmless-usage
@vindex harmless-usage-context-windows
@kindex g @r{(usage)}
@kbd{M-x harmless-usage} shows two blocks.

The session block is the context window of the current model, the
prompt size of the last request, and what remains in the window.  It
then shows the running prompt and completion totals for this chat.
Window sizes that Harmless does not learn from the provider live in
@code{harmless-usage-context-windows}.  A model with no known window
is marked unknown.

The account block is the last rate-limit remainder each provider sent.
A connection with no such headers says so.  For the connection named
@samp{xAI}, the buffer also asks xAI for the current allowance when
you are not in batch Emacs.  This report is not an account credit
balance.

In the usage buffer, @kbd{g} redraws and @kbd{q} quits.

@node Files
@chapter Files

@vindex harmless-directory
@cindex session files
Harmless keeps its own files under @code{harmless-directory}.  The
default is @file{harmless/} in @code{user-emacs-directory}.  Nil and
the empty string mean that same default.  A relative path is refused.
The filesystem root is refused.  A missing directory is created when
it is needed.  It is not replaced with @file{/}.

@table @file
@item config.el
Optional configuration, loaded once.

@item auth.json
@itemx auth-openai.json
@itemx auth-anthropic.json
@itemx auth-@var{slug}.json
OAuth tokens for each connection.  Mode 0600.

@item sessions/@var{project}/@var{id}/summary.json
The session's model, status, totals, and plan flag.

@item sessions/@var{project}/@var{id}/messages.jsonl
The transcript, one JSON object per line.

@item sessions/@var{project}/@var{id}/plan.md
The plan, when plan mode has written one.

@item sessions/_detached/@var{id}/
A chat with no project.

@item memory/global/
@itemx memory/workspaces/@var{project}/
Notes.  @xref{Memory}.
@end table

@var{project} in a session path is the project directory encoded as one
file name, not the project name you see in the buffer.

The log is not a file.  It is the buffer @code{*harmless-log*}.
@xref{Trouble}.

@node Keys
@chapter Keys

@findex harmless-menu
@kindex h @r{(Harmless buffers)}
@kbd{M-x harmless-menu} opens a transient with the common commands.
You can also open it with @kbd{m} in the transcript or @kbd{C-c C-k}
in the prompt.

@table @kbd
@item n
New session.

@item C
Detached chat.

@item c
Current project.

@item s
Switch session.

@item d
Dashboard.

@item a
Abort.

@item k
Compact the transcript.

@item P
Plan mode.

@item u
Usage.

@item m
Model.  In the transcript buffer, @kbd{m} opens this menu and
@kbd{M} picks a model directly.

@item e
Reasoning effort.

@item p
Permission mode.

@item l
Log in.

@item o
Log out.

@item r
Remember a note.

@item D
Dream: fold memory.

@item h
This manual.
@end table

@kbd{h} also opens this manual from the transcript, the dashboard, and
the usage buffer.

Dashboard keys:

@table @kbd
@item RET
Open the session on this line.

@item n
New session.

@item g
Refresh the list.

@item q
Quit.

@item h
This manual.
@end table

@node Trouble
@chapter Trouble

@findex harmless-abort
@findex harmless-reload-all-harmless
@cindex log
@kbd{M-x harmless-abort}, or @kbd{a} in the transcript, or @kbd{C-c C-a}
in the prompt, cancels the HTTP request or the shell command that is
running.  The session returns to idle and the transcript records
@samp{aborted}.

An empty reply from the model is an error: @samp{The model returned an
empty reply.}

Diagnostic lines go to the buffer @code{*harmless-log*}.  The buffer
keeps at most @code{harmless-log-max-lines} lines (2000).  Do not put
an API key in a note you expect to see there.  The log is not written
to disk.

@kbd{M-x harmless-reload-all-harmless} loads every @file{.el} file
beside the @file{harmless.el} Emacs actually loaded.  It skips
@file{harmless-autoloads.el} and prefers a newer source file over a
byte-compiled copy.  Restart Emacs when a live session was created
before a session field was added.  Reloading does not rebuild this
manual.  @xref{Manual}.

@node Manual
@chapter Manual

@findex harmless-info
@cindex Info manual
This book is an Info manual.  Open it with @kbd{M-x harmless-info},
with @kbd{h} in a Harmless buffer, or with @kbd{h} in the Harmless
menu.  After Harmless has loaded, @kbd{C-h i} lists it in the Emacs
directory.

The source is @file{doc/harmless.texi} next to @file{lisp}.  The file
Emacs reads is @file{doc/harmless.info}.  Rebuild it from the
repository root:

@example
make manual
@end example

That needs @command{makeinfo}, from the Texinfo package.  Commit both
the Texinfo source and the generated Info file.  A change to a
command, a key, a tool, or a permission belongs in the manual in the
same change.

